  <h3 class="text-xl mt-6 pb-1" id="chain-of-thought">
    Chain of Thought Prompting
  </h3>
  <p class="py-2">
    With chain of thaught prompting we provide an example reasoning process to
    the LLM. This way the LLM can learn to reason in a similar way.
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Prompt:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">
    Who has the most money?<br />
    Person 1: Starts with $100, then loses $40 and quadruples what is left.<br
    />
    Person 2: Starts with $50, then loses $10 and finally doubles what is left.<br
    /><br />
    Person 1 starts with $100<br />
    Loses $40, so they now have $100 - $40 = $60<br />
    Quadruples what is left, so they now have $60 * 4 = $240<br /><br />
    Person 2 starts with $50<br />
    Loses $10, so they now have $50 - $10 = $40<br />
    Doubles what is left, so they now have $40 * 2 = $80<br />
    So, Person 1 has the most money with $240.<br /><br />
    Who has the most money?<br />
    Person 1: Starts with $60, then loses $25 and triples what is left.<br />
    Person 2: Starts with $40, then loses $10 and finally quadruples what is left.
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Result:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">
    Person 1 starts with $60<br />
    Loses $25, so they now have $60 - $25 = $35<br />
    Triples what is left, so they now have $35 * 3 = $105<br /><br />
    Person 2 starts with $40<br />
    Loses $10, so they now have $40 - $10 = $30<br />
    Quadruples what is left, so they now have $30 * 4 = $120<br /><br />
    So, Person 2 has the most money with $120.
  </p>
  <h3 class="text-xl mt-6 pb-1" id="zero-shot-chain-of-thought">
    Zero Shot Chain of Thought Prompting
  </h3>
  <p class="py-2">
    With zero shot chain of thaught prompting we no longer provide a reasoning
    example, but simply ask the LLM to reason step by step.
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Prompt:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">
    There are two pizza's. One is 12 inches in diameter and the other is 16.
    What is the total area of the pizza's? Let's think step by step.
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Result:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">
    To calculate the area of a pizza, we need to know its diameter. Here's the
    step-by-step process:<br /><br />
    1. Find the radius of each pizza:<br />
    For the 12-inch pizza, the radius is half the diameter, so it is 12 / 2 = 6 inches.<br
    />
    For the 16-inch pizza, the radius is half the diameter, so it is 16 / 2 = 8 inches.<br
    /><br />
    2. Calculate the area of each pizza using the formula πr^2, where r is the radius:<br
    />
    For the 12-inch pizza, the area is π * 6^2 = π * 36 = 36π<br />
    For the 16-inch pizza, the area is π * 8^2 = π * 64 = 64π<br /><br />
    3. Add the areas of the two pizza's to find the total area:<br />
    Total area = 36π + 64π = 100π<br /><br />
    Note that π is a mathematical constant with a value of approximately 3.14, but
    in this calculation, it is used as an approximation. The exact value of the total
    area is 100 * 3.14 = 314 square inches.
  </p>
  <h3 class="text-xl mt-6 pb-1" id="exemplar-debiasing">Exemplar Debiasing</h3>
  <p class="py-2">
    Provding examples to an LLM can increase the quality of results, it can
    however also introduce bias. To prevent this we must make sure when using
    examples that we have an even distribution and order of types of prompts.
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Prompt:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">
    Q: I like to eat pizza.<br />
    A: positive<br /><br />
    Q: I read a book.<br />
    A: neutral<br /><br />
    Q: I am afraid of ghosts.<br />
    A: negative<br /><br />
    Q: I love going to the movies.<br />
    A: positive<br /><br />
    Q: I have biked to work.<br />
    A: neutral<br /><br />
    Q: I hate doing the dishes.<br />
    A: negative<br /><br />
    Q: I am shopping in the mall<br />
    A:
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Result:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">neutral</p>
  <h3 class="text-xl mt-6 pb-1" id="code-prompting">Code Prompting</h3>
  <p class="py-2">
    When using an LLM to write code, it is important to include what languages
    and stack you are using, what the user interface should look like, what
    functionality you are expecting and what responsing structure you are
    expecting.
  </p>
  <p class="py-2">Prompt:</p>
  <p class="bg-gray-900 text-slate-100 p-4 rounded-md font-mono">
    I am building a degree to radian converter in C++. The user can select to
    convert from degree to radian or vice versa and provides a number as input.
    The output needs to be rounded to two decimals. Provide comments when
    appropriate. The code should be written in a way that it can be easily
    extended to convert from other units.
  </p>
